# --- B1 intro ---
("""This chapter walks through the architecture of both PostgreSQL and SQL Server, covering how each engine receives queries, processes them, manages memory, writes data to disk, and handles concurrency. By the end, you will see both systems clearly enough to compare them and know exactly which internal component to investigate when something goes wrong in production.""",
 """This chapter walks through the architecture of SQL Server, covering how the engine receives queries, processes them, manages memory, writes data to disk, and handles concurrency. By the end, you will see the system clearly enough to know exactly which internal component to investigate when something goes wrong in production."""),

# --- B2 postmaster -> SQLOS connection architecture ---
("""In <strong class="text-white">PostgreSQL</strong>, the process begins with the <strong class="text-white">postmaster</strong>, the parent process that listens on the network port (typically 5432) and spawns a dedicated backend process for each incoming client connection. This is a process-per-connection model. Each backend is an independent operating system process, which means a crash in one backend cannot corrupt the memory of another. The backend receives the query string and passes it through four stages: parsing, rewriting, planning, and execution.""",
 """In <strong class="text-white">SQL Server</strong>, everything runs inside a single service process (<code class="BG">sqlservr.exe</code>), scheduled by a user-mode operating system layer called <strong class="text-white">SQLOS</strong>. When a client connects — typically over the TDS (Tabular Data Stream) protocol on port 1433 — SQLOS assigns the connection to a scheduler (one per logical CPU) and lends it a worker thread from the thread pool. This thread-pool model means a thousand idle connections cost far less than a thousand OS processes would, but it also means worker-thread exhaustion is a real failure mode: when all workers are busy, new requests queue behind the <code class="BG">THREADPOOL</code> wait, and the instance stops accepting work even though CPU looks idle.""".replace('class="BG"', 'class="%s"' % C)),

# --- B3 parser/rewriter/planner -> SQL Server pipeline depth ---
("""The <strong class="text-white">parser</strong> converts the SQL text into an internal tree structure called a parse tree. The <strong class="text-white">rewriter</strong> applies any rewrite rules — most commonly, it expands views by substituting their definition into the query. The <strong class="text-white">planner</strong> (also called the optimizer) takes the rewritten query tree and generates one or more candidate execution plans, assigns cost estimates to each using statistics from <code class="BG">pg_statistic</code>, and selects the cheapest one. Finally, the <strong class="text-white">executor</strong> runs the chosen plan and streams rows back to the client.""".replace('class="BG"', 'class="%s"' % C),
 """The query text then travels a pipeline with four stages. The <strong class="text-white">parser</strong> checks syntax and builds a parse tree. The <strong class="text-white">algebrizer</strong> (also called the binder) resolves every object name, column reference, and data type against the system catalog, producing a logical operator tree. The <strong class="text-white">query optimizer</strong> — a cost-based Cascades-framework optimizer — explores equivalent plan shapes (join orders, join algorithms, access methods), estimates each candidate's cost using statistics from <code class="BG">sys.stats</code> and cardinality estimation, and picks the cheapest. Finally, the <strong class="text-white">execution engine</strong> runs the chosen plan and streams rows back to the client.""".replace('class="BG"', 'class="%s"' % C)),

