# --- B5 plan cache comparison -> SQL Server depth ---
("""A key practical difference emerges here: PostgreSQL historically compiled a new plan for each execution of an ad-hoc query (though prepared statements cache plans), while SQL Server aggressively caches execution plans. This makes plan cache management an important SQL Server DBA task that has no direct equivalent in PostgreSQL day-to-day operations.""",
 """A key practical characteristic of the engine: SQL Server caches execution plans aggressively, which makes plan cache management a core DBA responsibility. Ad-hoc workloads can bloat the cache with thousands of single-use plans — the <code class="BG">optimize for ad hoc workloads</code> setting stores only a plan stub on first execution and the full plan on second use. And cached plans have a dark side: <strong class="text-white">parameter sniffing</strong>, where a plan optimized for one parameter value performs terribly for another, is one of the most common sources of "the query was fast yesterday" incidents. Query Store, covered later in this book, is the flight recorder that lets you prove it.""".replace('class="BG"', 'class="%s"' % C)),

